course: everyday
lesson: 19
title: Your uh-oh feeling
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your body has its own alarm: when something seems wrong, you may feel butterflies in your tummy, a fast heartbeat, sweaty hands or a tight throat — an «uh-oh» feeling.
explain: This feeling is your brain noticing danger signs, sometimes before you can say what they are.
explain: When you get it, move away, say «no» if you need to, and tell a trusted adult — even if it turns out to be nothing.
explain: It is okay to say no to any adult, even one you know, if what they ask makes you feel unsafe; being safe matters more than being polite.
explain: A surprise, like a birthday present, is happy and will soon be shared; a secret that makes you feel worried or uncomfortable should always be told to a trusted adult.
explain: Example: in a park in Phoenix, a girl feels uh-oh when a man she does not know keeps asking her to help find his puppy; she says no, walks to her mum and tells her.
explain: A girl in Ohio felt uneasy when an older boy kept asking her to come to an empty place; she ignored the feeling and went, got frightened, and ran back. Next time she listened to the feeling first.
explain: A boy in Auckland had an uh-oh feeling when friends dared him to cross a railway line; he said no and walked away, and later he heard a train had come through very fast.
fact: Fear makes the heart beat faster, breathing quicken and muscles get ready to move.
fact: The uh-oh feeling can come before you understand what is wrong.
fact: Trusting your uh-oh feeling and moving away is never wrong, even if it was nothing.
fact: You can say no to anyone, even an adult you know, if something feels unsafe.
fact: Adults who need help usually ask other adults, not children.
fact: A secret that makes you feel bad or worried should be told to a trusted adult.
fact: A surprise is something nice that everyone will find out about soon.
fact: Telling a trusted adult about something that worried you is always the right thing to do.
fact: Trusted adults are people like parents, carers, teachers and school counsellors.
fact: If the first adult you tell does not help, keep telling other trusted adults until someone does.
fact: New but safe things, like a first swimming lesson, can also bring a nervous feeling; talking about it helps you tell the difference.
fact: People who ignore their uh-oh feeling sometimes walk into danger they could have avoided.
fact: You never need a reason to say no and leave when something feels wrong.
fact: When people say two adults are "seeing" each other, they mean the two are a couple, going out together; that is their own private business.
fact: When a couple are "splitting up", they are ending their time together; parents who split up may divorce, which means ending their marriage.
fact: "You and Sophie sitting in a tree" is a teasing playground rhyme that says two children are a couple; you can ask the teaser to stop and tell an adult if it goes on.
fact: Worried feelings about news at home are normal, and talking to a trusted adult about them helps.
exercise: What can the uh-oh feeling feel like in your body? | A fast heartbeat and butterflies in the tummy | A sudden heavy wish to fall asleep straight away | A strong, hungry rumble in the tummy | Wanting to laugh at nothing
exercise: A teenager you met online asks to meet you alone and to keep it secret. You feel uh-oh. What do you do? | Say no and tell a trusted adult | Go, but take a friend along with you | Keep it secret, as you were asked | Ask more questions to be sure first
exercise: A neighbour you know asks you to come into his house alone, and you feel uneasy. Is it okay to say no? | Yes, you can say no to anyone if you feel unsafe | No, you must obey every adult you know, even if you feel bad | Only if your parents are watching you | Only if he is not really a neighbour
exercise: What is the difference between a surprise and a worrying secret? | A surprise is happy and soon shared; a worrying secret makes you feel bad | A surprise is always longer than a secret and has to be kept for weeks | A worrying secret is always about presents | There is no real difference between them
exercise: A grown-up you do not know asks you to help find his lost dog. Why should you feel uh-oh? | Adults who need help usually ask other adults | Dogs never really get lost | Helping animals is against the law for children | Strangers never have pets of their own, so he must be lying
exercise: You told an adult about something that worried you, and it was nothing. What does that mean? | That is fine; telling was the right choice | You should feel silly and never tell again | You will be punished for wasting time | Next time you should wait until you are sure
exercise: Your heart races because someone keeps following you home. What should you do? | Go to a busy place or a safe adult and tell them | Walk more slowly to see if they pass you | Hide somewhere quiet where nobody at all can see you | Turn around and argue with the person
exercise: Why is being safe more important than being polite? | Your safety matters more than not upsetting someone | Being rude is always a good thing to do, whoever it is | Polite children are never safe anywhere | Adults like rude children better
exercise: You told your teacher something that worried you, but nothing happened. What next? | Keep telling other trusted adults | Give up and keep it to yourself | Sort it out all on your own | Tell only your little brother
exercise: A friend makes you promise not to tell that someone is hurting her. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult, because she needs help | Keep the promise, whatever happens | Tell the other children at school | Wait until she says it is okay to tell anybody about it
exercise: You feel uh-oh before your first jump from a high diving board. What could help? | Talk with your coach about the feeling | Jump straight away without telling anyone at all | Climb even higher to beat the fear | Pretend that you feel nothing
exercise: Why can the uh-oh feeling come before you know what is wrong? | Your brain notices danger signs very fast | The feeling is random and means nothing | Your tummy can see what your eyes cannot | It only comes when you are hungry
exercise: Who is a trusted adult? | A parent, carer, teacher or school counsellor | Anyone who gives you a present or sweets | Any adult who knows your name | An adult you met online who seems kind and friendly
exercise: An older cousin shows you something that feels wrong and says, «Don't tell.» What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep quiet because he is family | Tell him you will think about it | Tell only if it happens again
test: At a bus stop in Vancouver, a man keeps standing too close to Emma and her stomach knots. What is the best choice? | Move away towards people and tell an adult | Stay still so as not to seem rude | Smile and hope that he leaves soon | Ignore the feeling, as he has done nothing
test: Which is true? | It is fine to say no to an adult when you feel unsafe | Children must always do what adults say, whatever happens | You may say no only to strangers | Feelings in your tummy are never useful
test: Max's uncle gives him a present and says, «Don't tell your mum, it's our secret.» Max feels uneasy. What should he do? | Tell his mum or another trusted adult | Keep the secret because it was a gift | Tell only his best friend about it | Give the present back and say nothing
test: Your class is planning a surprise party for your teacher. Should you tell her? | No, it is a happy surprise that will soon be shared | Yes, every secret must be told at once | Yes, because surprises are worrying secrets that must be told | No, and you must keep it secret for ever
test: Which body signals can be the uh-oh feeling? | A racing heart, a tight throat, sweaty hands | Yawning, heavy eyelids and wanting to sleep | A rumbling, hungry tummy | A runny nose and sneezing
test: What could happen if you never listened to your uh-oh feeling? | You might stay too long where it is unsafe | You would always be safer than others, because you are brave | You would never feel afraid again | Nothing could ever go wrong for you
test: A car pulls up and the driver leans out to ask Sophie for directions. She feels uh-oh. What should she do? | Step back, stay out of reach and walk away | Lean into the car to point on his map for him | Get in to show him the right way | Walk right up to the driver's window
test: Why should you keep telling adults if the first one does not help? | You deserve to be helped and kept safe | Adults like to hear a story many times | There is a rule that you must tell three people | The first adult is always the wrong one
test: Noah feels uh-oh before his first school play, though nothing is dangerous. What is going on? | New things can bring a nervous feeling that is safe | It means the play is dangerous | It means he should run away from school before it starts | It means he is ill and must go home
test: man in a car asked me to help find his puppy | Say no and move away, adults ask adults | Help him, he sounds sad and it's only a puppy | Get in the car to look | Ask him to show you photos
test: uncle says it's our secret and not to tell, feels bad | Tell a trusted adult | Keep it, he's family | Only tell your friends | Forget about it
test: got a funny feeling about that bloke, prob nothing tho | Move away anyway, it's never wrong | Stay, it's rude to just walk off from people | Wait until you're sure | Ask him what he wants
test: told my teacher, she didn't help | Keep telling other trusted adults | Stop telling anyone | Keep it to yourself now, grown-ups don't care | Sort it out on your own
test: something feels really off about this, but they'll call me a baby | Trust the feeling, leave and tell an adult | Stay so they do not laugh | Prove you are brave and keep going whatever happens | Wait until it gets worse
test: grown-up i don't know keeps asking me to help him behind the shop | Say no, go to a busy place and tell an adult | Go, he might really need help, it's kind to help | Go if he seems polite | Go only for a minute
test: Kai says u need proof before u leave a bad situation. right? | No, an uh-oh feeling is enough to leave | Yes, always wait until you're really sure | Yes, leaving is rude | Yes, unless someone tells you
test: heard a girl in year 6 got in a car with a lady who said her mum was in hospital, it was all fake | Whatever the details, the lesson is real: adults who need help ask adults, and trust your uh-oh feeling | It proves ladies are never safe to talk to | It means you should go if they know your mum | It is gossip, so the uh-oh feeling does not matter
test: my mate says if u get an uh-oh feeling and it turns out to be nothing ur a baby and should be embarrassed | That is wrong; moving away on an uh-oh feeling is never wrong, even if it was nothing | He is right, you should wait until you are completely sure something bad is happening | He is right, only babies feel uh-oh | He is right, so ignore it next time
test: Leah told the teacher a secret about her uncle and now everyone's whispering about her | Do not whisper or spread it; she did the right thing telling a trusted adult | Spread it, since everyone in the class knows about it anyway, so it's fine | Spread it, so the secret gets to her uncle | Spread it, and guess what the secret was
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between a secret and a surprise? | A surprise is a pleasant matter soon to be revealed, whereas a worrying secret should be told to a trusted adult | A secret and a surprise are identical in every respect | A surprise must never be revealed, whereas a secret must be told immediately to all | A secret is always pleasant, whereas a surprise is always worrying
exam: It being given that the first adult informed of a worrying matter renders no assistance, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To continue informing other trusted adults until assistance is rendered | To keep the matter secret thereafter | To resolve the matter alone without further help from any adult whatsoever | To inform only other children of the matter
exam: Identify the correct statement concerning a child's entitlement to refuse an adult. | A child may refuse any adult, including one known to him, where something feels unsafe | A child may refuse only adults who are strangers | A child may refuse an adult only with a written reason | A child may never refuse an adult under any circumstance
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the physical effects of fear. | The heart beats faster, breathing quickens and the muscles prepare for movement | The heart slows considerably and the muscles become entirely relaxed and limp | Breathing ceases until the danger has passed | No change in the body is produced by fear
test: big kid said Miss Parker's "seeing" the PE teacher n told me to spread it round, feels wrong | They are probably a couple, which is their private business, so do not spread it | It means she watches him do PE, so tell everyone | It means they are in trouble, so tell the head teacher at once | It means nothing, so make up more and pass it on
test: heard mum on the phone whispering "they're splitting up, don't tell the kids", now my tummy feels funny | Someone's parents may be divorcing; it is ok to feel worried, so talk to mum or another trusted adult | It means they are moving house, nothing to feel | It means you are in trouble, so hide | It means you must tell all your friends right away
test: older boy keeps saying "ooh you and Sophie sitting in a tree" and won't stop, feels yucky | He is teasing that you two are a couple; you can tell him to stop and tell an adult if he goes on | It means you and Sophie must climb a tree together | It means you have to stop being friends with Sophie | It is a compliment, so ask him to say it louder
